# Implementation Plan: Google Discovery

**Branch**: `002-google-discovery` | **Date**: 2026-08-27 | **Spec**: [spec.md](./spec.md)  
**Input**: Approved, clarified, and upgraded Google Discovery specification  
**Status**: Design complete; ready for task generation

## Summary

Build one reusable discovery layer on top of the existing Next.js and Supabase storefront. Stable product, collection, and content identities will produce real locale-specific routes; page metadata, JSON-LD, breadcrumbs, sitemap entries, redirects, and future Merchant feeds will consume those same facts. Organic improvements ship now. Merchant endpoints exist but fail closed with 404 until the final domain, seller ownership, market, shipping, and return facts are approved.

## Technical Context

**Language/Version**: TypeScript 6.0.x strict mode; SQL/PostgreSQL migrations; Node.js 24 LTS  
**Primary Dependencies**: Next.js 16.3.2 App Router, React 19.2, Supabase JS/SSR 2.112.4, next-intl 4.13.7, Zod 4.4.3  
**Storage**: Existing Supabase Postgres catalog/content tables and approved public media renditions; no new external store  
**Testing**: Vitest 4.1.11, Testing Library, Playwright 1.62.1, Supabase pgTAP, generated metadata/sitemap/feed contract tests  
**Target Platform**: Netlify-hosted server-rendered web storefront backed by managed Supabase; local placeholder origin for development  
**Project Type**: Single localized ecommerce web application  
**Performance Goals**: Preserve p75 LCP ≤2.5 seconds, INP <200 milliseconds, CLS <0.1; sitemap readiness below 45,000 URLs  
**Constraints**: No final domain, Search Console property, Merchant account, approved seller mapping, or finalized market policies; no external activation; public claims must be verified; no new analytics provider  
**Scale/Scope**: Four locales, three storefront currencies, initial Georgia and Germany/EU feed profiles, one sitemap until the 45,000-URL guard requires sharding

## Constitution Check

| Principle | Evidence | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Specification before construction | Upgraded spec has five independent journeys, 34 requirements, edge cases, and measurable outcomes | PASS |
| Product truth and commerce correctness | All search outputs reuse guarded price, inventory, media, publication, and explicit identifier facts; incomplete Merchant facts fail closed | PASS |
| Customer-first accessible design | Collector’s Index breadcrumbs and collection index are semantic, localized, mobile-first, keyboard-operable, and reviewed at 390/768/1440px | PASS |
| Security and privacy | XML contains public facts only; private routes use response/document no-index; structured data is safely serialized | PASS |
| Tested buying journeys | Unit, database, output, crawl, accessibility, visual, performance, and build checks are planned | PASS |
| Simplicity and incremental delivery | Existing models are extended and one route-set contract feeds every discovery consumer | PASS |

Post-design, all gates remain PASS. No external service or unsupported business claim is added. Merchant activation remains separate and blocked on evidence.

## Architecture and Reuse

### Shared discovery truth

`features/seo/routes.ts` introduces `LocalizedRouteSet`. Query functions load all published translations for one stable identity and build a canonical, real alternates, `x-default`, and indexability decision. Product, collection, journal, service metadata, JSON-LD, sitemap, and feed landing links use the route set instead of rebuilding paths independently.

### Catalog projection

An append-only migration extends products with `brand`, `gtin`, `mpn`, and `identifier_exists`, exposes translation SEO fields through `public_catalog_products`, and adds a published-collection projection. Existing search, price, inventory, and approved media behavior stays authoritative.

### Metadata and structured data

`features/catalog/metadata.ts` becomes the common metadata/JSON-LD boundary. It uses a branded fallback social image, makes fallback pages `noindex,follow`, emits Product only when the eligibility gate and minimum facts pass, and provides WebSite/OnlineStore, Article, and Breadcrumb builders. Optional seller, origin, shipping, returns, and identifiers are omitted unless explicit approved inputs exist.

### Crawlable storefront

A localized `app/[locale]/(store)/collections/page.tsx` lists published collections. `components/storefront/breadcrumbs.tsx` provides semantic navigation to collection, product, journal article, and service pages. Header/footer fallback links point to `/collections`. Search and filtered collection requests remain `noindex,follow`; clean curated collections remain canonical.

### Sitemap and redirects

`app/sitemap.ts` groups translations by stable identity, includes eligible surfaces and approved images, uses source timestamps, and throws a readiness error at 45,000 URLs. Existing `content_redirects` is reused for audited slug redirects. Root navigation uses `permanentRedirect` for HTTP 308.

### Merchant feeds

`features/seo/merchant-feed.ts` defines strict profiles and feed-item validation. `app/feeds/google/[profile]/route.ts` recognizes `ge-en-gel`, `de-de-eur`, `de-en-eur`, and `de-ru-eur`. Every profile initially remains disabled with readiness errors, so endpoints return 404. Builders and XML contracts are tested; activation requires explicit facts and evidence.

### Operations and content

`docs/operations/google-discovery-activation.md` records domain, Search Console, Merchant, Bing, rich-result, sitemap, feed, and monitoring steps. `docs/seo/keyword-page-map.md` is a four-locale planning ledger limited to verified inventory and genuine buyer needs, never generated landing pages.

